WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.440
Let June the 23rd go down in our history

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.960
as our independence day!

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.760
The British people have made a very

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.080
clear decision to take a different path.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.120
No one really knows what the
future is going to look like.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.120
That's a defacement of the Union Jack!

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.640
You're not allowed to deface our flag

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.880
The shocking defeat
of the Remain campaign,

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.760
"We've got our country back"

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.560
Caused the largest collapse
of any major currency

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.920
in the history of the modern
global financial system.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.880
You could literally watch the pound

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.960
falling off the edge of a cliff.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.160
The last time we saw falls of 8%,

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.600
I'm not sure we actually know.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.800
People in the City
of London were running

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.680
their own private polling
to get information

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.400
about the vote while
it was still happening.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.480
It was probably one of
the most lucrative nights

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.160
for the hedge fund industry
that there had ever been.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.240
My name's Gavin Finch, I'm a
journalist at Bloomberg News.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.480
I cover financial crime.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.680
I'm Cam Simpson, I'm an
investigations editor and writer.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.120
My name is Kit Chellel,

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:11.440
I'm a reporter with Bloomberg,

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:13.520
and a writer for Businessweek.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:14.880
And we're the three reporters

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.440
who broke the Brexit Big Short.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.320
The Brexit night was a huge deal

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.720
for everyone in the country.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.920
I watched it on television
the same way most people did.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.040
At 10pm voting stopped,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.600
the polling stations closed.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.440
All of the networks,
all of the broadcasters

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:32.320
went immediately to their coverage.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.880
In or out. It is too late to change your mind now.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.560
So within the first
minute they had a concession

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.120
from Nigel Farage who's
the global face of Brexit.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.880
This statement,
as the polls close,

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.240
"It's been an extraordinary
referendum campaign,"

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.000
this is Nigel Farage, "turnout looks to be

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.960
exceptionally high and looks
like Remain will edge it."

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.280
I remember being shocked
when I heard that just

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.560
because it was quite out of
character for somebody like him

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.480
to concede before even the
results had been counted.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.440
Later that evening, Nigel Farage came

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.440
out again with a second concession speech,

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.720
and this one was a bit more
specific than the first.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.080
In this one he said that Remain had won,

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.440
and that he had heard
this from some friends

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.200
in the City who run some
pretty big private polling.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.160
And that really shook me.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.520
That was when I really
realized, wait a minute.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.840
The pollsters secretly
working with hedge funds,

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.880
and Nigel had access 
to that information,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.560
before any of the votes
were even announced.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.200
That was the moment when I realized

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.240
there's a lot here for us to look into.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.040
First thing that I did was
I created a very detailed

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.000
timeline of everything
that happened politically,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.480
everything that the pollsters were doing

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.320
and how the markets were reacting.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.720
We had to try to identify
the polling firms that were

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.200
involved and the hedge funds
that they were working for.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.800
So we had these two giant haystacks

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.720
that we kinda had to go
searching for needles in.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.000
We call this a mosaic investigation.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.120
You gather all the pieces that you can

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.320
and you build a mosaic.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:14.240
It's not a perfect picture,
but it's a good picture

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.360
and one that you can kind
of see what happened.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.720
So we set out to interview as many

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.200
of the polling company employees,

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.840
former employees we started with

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.880
so as not to alert them
to what we were doing.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:31.200
And people were genuinely
scared about the implications

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.320
of talking to a journalist
about something like this.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.280
We basically wanted
to know who the buyers were

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.480
of these polls and who
was conducting them and why.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.640
Two polling firms that
were notable in all this,

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.080
and that we highlighted in the story,

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.000
were YouGov which is one
of the fastest-growing

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.960
polling companies in the world,

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.520
and a company called Survation.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.000
Hello and welcome to Pop Idol.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.120
In the early stages of
both YouGov and Survation,

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.840
part of their market legend
was built around the work

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:08.840
they did on U.K. talent competitions

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:11.480
like Pop Idol and X Factor.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.640
They were really the first
to build polling panels,

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:23.040
large groups of people that
were available to them online

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.160
that they could tap with sort
of the press of a button.

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:28.080
What was interesting about those polls

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.120
is it was an early indicator
that you didn't need to know

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:34.240
the outcome of the actual competition.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.480
You didn't need to know who won X Factor.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.320
All you needed to know
was who was gonna vote

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:43.240
for which candidate on which
particular week in X Factor,

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:46.200
for you to then be able
to make a market bet.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.400
For the Brexit referendum,
the senior members

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.680
of the polling industry told
the press that it was not

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.040
possible to do a regular
exit poll for this event.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.000
Their normal methods,
their normal data gathering

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.640
simply wouldn't work in this context.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:06.440
What they didn't say
was they were secretly

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:09.400
doing it for hedge funds 
at the same time,

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.800
who were trying to 
profit on the day

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:14.760
from the ultimate inside information

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.000
which was an exit poll showing
how British people had voted.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:22.960
Hedge funds are among the

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.600
most sophisticated players
in financial markets.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:27.960
What they're looking for is an edge,

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.160
some sort of insight that no one else has

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:33.080
that they can use to make
their trading profits

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:34.360
and make money for their investors.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.720
If the markets were
deciding this referendum,

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.480
they would already have
called it for Remain.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.200
The pound is absolutely
surging on the markets.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:44.240
But the pound kept going to new highs

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:45.680
all throughout the evening.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.360
If you're a hedge fund
with access to information

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:53.240
showing you that actually, the
opposite is likely to happen,

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.360
then you're in an incredibly good place

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:59.000
because you can play that market
all the way up to the top.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.400
Short selling is essentially
a bet in a given direction.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.320
The bet is that something
will fall in value.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.320
So the higher the pound is pushed up,

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:07.720
the more money you make when it

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.000
comes back down the other side.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.720
Hedge funds and banks
were willing to pay

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:14.480
comparatively enormous 
sums for the data

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:16.240
that polling firms were selling

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.640
because it allowed them
to trade so profitably.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:21.360
$100 million, $160 million,

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.400
$300 million in a single day.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.120
When you're looking at the
definition of insider trading,

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:31.520
insider trading is when you have access to

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:36.600
and trade on non-public,
market-moving information.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:38.880
In this country, exit polling data is

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.680
criminally defined as
non-public information.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:44.480
It is a crime to provide it to the public

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:48.360
or any section of the public
before the polls are closed.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.560
Polling companies were
streaming it all day long

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:52.720
to their secret hedge fund clients

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.560
long before the polls were closed

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:56.440
so that they could trade on how people

00:06:56.440 --> 00:06:59.160
were voting while they were still voting.

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:04.040
I hope and pray that my
sense of this tonight is wrong.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:05.600
Nigel Farage does seem to think

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:07.480
that the referendum is lost.

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.920
The results as they've been
coming through this evening,

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.320
rather I think cast doubt, very
early stage, on Mr. Farage's

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.960
rather low-key approach
to what's going on.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:20.120
So on the evening of the Brexit vote

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:21.440
after the markets had closed,

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.360
the concession speeches which Nigel gave

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.920
certainly did drive markets higher.

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:31.760
What we wanted to understand was

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.760
why did he concede that night?

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.680
What information did he have
when he made those concessions?

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:38.800
What was going through his mind?

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:42.000
And whether he had access to
some of the private polling

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.760
that we had identified that
was being done by hedge funds?

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.920
So we really had to
figure out a way to try

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:49.320
and get to the bottom of some of this

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:52.880
and we asked Nigel to
come to lunch with us.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:58.640
So we met Nigel Farge at Sweetings.

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:00.640
That was on Kit's suggestion

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:02.440
and it was a good one
because it turns out that

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.840
Nigel was the third
generation of his family

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.640
who had been eating at Sweetings.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:11.400
It's kind of a strange venue.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:14.160
It feels like stepping
back into the 1950s.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.960
We went in
through the door with him.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:18.800
And he immediately had a go at the

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.120
Spanish host and waiter for being Spanish.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.560
I'd never eaten oysters before,

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:27.720
and Nigel Farage insisted
that I try oysters.

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:31.120
About halfway through the meal,

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:35.560
our attention certainly
changed to our story.

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:38.560
And we started asking him
about his concession speeches.

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:41.120
If we'd just tried to call
him up and ask those questions,

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:42.480
he would have hung up the phone.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.880
Or if we knocked on his door,
he would have shut the door.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:48.080
But he felt, I think,
compelled at that point

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.240
to give us full answers and we got them.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:59.480
He certainly confirmed
to us that in between

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:01.800
at least the beginning
of the first concession

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:04.440
and the second concession,
he had spoken to

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.840
Damien Lyons Lowe at Survation.

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:09.960
Damien Lyons Lowe is the
founder and the CEO of Survation.

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:13.160
He built Survation's
reputation in the early days

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:18.160
by polling in a way that
drove UKIP's results higher.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:19.800
That immediately brought him

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:21.440
to the attention of Nigel Farage

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:25.200
and he became Nigel Farage's
pollster and UKIP's pollster,

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:28.000
and a very key political
advisor for Nigel Farage.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:32.080
Nigel knew from his favorite pollster

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:34.480
Leave had won before he gave at least

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.920
one of his concessions
that really pushed the

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:38.920
market in the wrong direction that night.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:41.160
So that was pretty critical
for us to find out.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:44.120
On the night that he
gave the concession speech,

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:47.240
we do know there were
others within UKIP and

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:52.960
within the Leave EU campaign
who were very curious

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:53.000
within the Leave EU campaign
who were very curious
as to why he was going on air
saying that Leave had lost

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:59.320
as to why he was going on air
saying that Leave had lost

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:02.960
because they too had access
to this private polling data

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:03.920
which showed that Leave had won.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:08.160
The reasons he gave to us
that he made those concessions

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:11.800
were simply that he had an
attack of nerves, essentially.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:14.120
When the moment came he got jittery,

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:15.480
and nervous, and lost faith.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:19.280
He described being in a sort
of black despair and a gloom

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:22.760
about what the outcome
would be and losing hope.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:24.520
What his personal dealings were

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:28.040
we're not sure of but we're still looking.

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:32.120
This whole process was us building

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:34.480
a very complicated jigsaw from scratch

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:36.200
and it perhaps wasn't right until the end

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:38.720
of the reporting process
that we really saw

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:41.080
the whole picture in all its complexity.

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:42.440
The fallout was massive.

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:44.760
I mean we were really
surprised that a story about

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:47.840
derivatives, essentially, was like

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:50.320
the third biggest
trending thing on Twitter.

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:55.000
And it didn't take long 
for U.K. lawmakers

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:58.600
in both Houses of
Parliament to start calling

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:01.080
for official inquiries into this.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:04.400
It matters to different
people for different reasons.

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:06.640
It matters if you're a
trader because it matters

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:09.400
if someone else has an
unfair advantage over you.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:12.480
It matters if you care
that a handful of people

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:18.200
can make huge sums of money
by betting on political events

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:21.760
that shape our future and
using information that would be

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:23.800
illegal for you or I to look at.

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:27.800
I think it matters if
politicians are involved in this

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:29.840
and we should know more about it.

